    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Libertarians argue that taxation represents a violation of individual property rights and self-ownership, asserting that the state has no moral authority to force contributions without unanimous consent. In contrast, social democrats contend that absolute rights do not justify extreme inequality or the complete rejection of those unable to sustain themselves due to societal failures. The core moral tension lies in whether the collective good justifies restricting individual economic freedom through the fair share of mandatory taxation. You will need to polemicize on the \"slippery slope\" of state overreach versus the \"path of least resistance\" toward welfare state integration.",
